  abstract: >-
    Harmful pesticide effects exceeding specific protection goals (SPG) may go
    undetected in underpowered studies. Regulatory honeybee field studies have
    consistently failed to reach the statistical power required under European
    Food Safety Authority (EFSA) guidance, which may have contributed to the
    approval of high-risk substances. Therefore, EFSA advised a shift from testing
    the null hypothesis of 'no effect' (difference testing) to equivalence testing.
    Under this new approach, a pesticide is classified as 'low risk' if the null
    hypothesis that its effect exceeds the SPG can be rejected. For honeybees, the
    recommended SPG is a colony size reduction below 10%. Critics have argued that
    this framework requires excessive experimental effort to demonstrate pesticide
    safety and proposed an alternative equivalence test defining treatment effects
    relative to the lower bound of the 90%-control-group confidence interval. Using
    simulations mimicking a regulatory honeybee field study, we show that although
    the two equivalence tests share the same trade-off between false 'low-risk' and
    false 'high-risk' classifications, only EFSA's equivalence test reliably
    identifies pesticides with effects >SPG at alpha = 0.2. Our results show that
    increasing sample sizes beyond current practice is often necessary for reliable
    regulatory assessment. However, for pesticides with effect sizes <=5%, sample
    size requirements remain lower than those implied by the power requirement of
    EFSA's former difference-test-based guidance. Moreover, covariate adjustment -
    through a model term or balanced colony allocation using anticlustering
    randomisation - can reduce sample size requirements without losing power and
    thus save costs. Finally, we provide practical guidance for anticlustering
    randomisation, equivalence testing and their implementation in R.
